hi there steve kaufman here it's  american thanksgiving day and uh since  i'm in palm springs  uh i will be attending uh for the first  time in my life an american thanksgiving  party but today i'm going to talk about  grammar and and ways of teaching or  learning grammar and i'm gonna compare  exercises and drills and questions to  simply  patterns  um i'm uh nearing the end of my polish  challenge it's the 26th at the end of  november it'll be 90 days  i'll do a little video video interview  if i can with one of my polish tutors  i've been talking to polish tutors on  italki one of them  she said she asked me what problems do  you have with polish and i mentioned a  few including of course  uh verbs of motion which is always an  issue with slavic languages so she  prepared a lesson on verbs of motion  and she had you know exercises and  please complete the questions and so i  was doing this in my discussion with her  if you know make sure you put in the  right verb and all this kind of stuff  and so we did this for an hour  and then after the  session  then of course and i get as usual a list  of of my main errors uh during the  lesson as well as these uh questions  that she had given me  so i import these into links so that i  can review the words and phrases  and i kind of came  my feeling and i'd be interested in  other people's reactions my feeling is  that the exercise by the way i'm sitting  on one of these exercise balls that's  why i'm bouncing up and down i hope that  doesn't disturb you too much  um  the uh hour spent  trying to come up with the right  verb and trying to answer the questions  and complete the sentences  i think didn't help me very much i don't  retain very much i kind of feel that  there is pressure on me to to answer to  complete the sentence to and and a lot  of this pressure  actually is negative insofar as learning  is concerned when i see  um you know a list of sentences with  different verbs of motion or if people  can actually make a little story with  different verbs of motion and i can read  these and listen to them  and save them at link and see them again  then gradually i will get used to them  you can't teach something like this it's  not because we had a lesson and i  answered questions that i've moved along  that much in my opinion in this process  of learning something in fact  it may be that some of the other issues  that came up in our uh discussion my  case endings maybe i picked up on some  of that  rather than what she was deliberately  trying to teach me so i just i just have  this feeling it almost confirms me that  not to mention the fact that i prefer  just to have a conversation and then to  get this list of words and phrases which  i review which is also this very gradual  layering combined with the listening  that i'm doing interesting listening  that i'm doing about history  occasionally reviewing filters simple  stories and there's this just gradual  layering and accumulation and and that  this is pleasant and this is  slowly effective but that's deliberate  testing and answering this question and  completing the sentence is less pleasant  more stressful and probably less  effective when it comes even to learning  the issues of grammar which will  eventually slot in if we are determined  to learn them and and along the way we  occasionally will consult a table or  look up some rules but mostly just  listen and read notice and gradually we  improve so that's my take on grammar  sort of deliberate grammar instruction  versus versus learning grammar through  exposure to to a group of sentences you  know  concentrated examples of a certain  pattern and or then just noticing them  in our listening and reading so i look  forward to your  comments and the next and last uh video  in this whole  polish challenge  series will be an interview with one of  my tutors thank you for listening bye  for now
 

